Thomas Kirkbride was a quaker, born in the Pennsylvania countryside in 1809. His father was a farmer, but young Kirkbride discovered early on that he wasn't suited to farming, and he decided to become a doctor. At first he wanted to be a surgeon. Then he got kind of randomly talked into working instead at a Quaker run in St. Asylum outside of Philadelphia. A quick note about language. Historically, words that may sound offensive now, like insane or lunatic, would have been commonplace in the late 18th and early 19th century, even used by medical professionals. So for clarity's sake, we'll be using the terminology that Kirkbride would have used.
托马斯·柯克布赖德是一位贵格会教徒，1809年出生于宾夕法尼亚州的乡村。他的父亲是一名农民，但年轻的柯克布赖德很早就发现自己不适合务农，于是决定成为一名医生。起初他想成为一名外科医生。后来，他被随机说服去费城郊外的一家贵格会经营的精神病院工作。关于语言的一个简短说明：从历史上看，像“疯狂的”或“精神错乱的”这样现在听起来可能带有冒犯性的词汇，在18世纪末和19世纪初是司空见惯的，甚至被医学专业人士使用。因此，为了清晰起见，我们将使用柯克布赖德当时会使用的术语。

