00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:37.000
Wealthy families often kept in stain relatives at home or paid for them to live in private mad houses. Poor people fared worse. Some of them were kind of cast out to walk along roads. A lot of them ended up in county jails. A lot of them ended up in some kind of, you know, small, privately run alms house. What mad houses did exist were more like prisons, largely focused on confining and often torturing people. In some, such as England's infamous Bedlam, the public could even pay to come
富裕家庭通常把精神失常的亲属留在家里，或者花钱让他们住在私人的疯人院里。穷人的境遇则更糟。有些人被赶出去在路上流浪。许多人最终进了县监狱。许多人最终进了某种小型、私人经营的济贫院。当时存在的疯人院更像是监狱，主要集中在监禁和经常折磨人。在一些地方，比如英国臭名昭著的贝特莱姆皇家医院，公众甚至可以付费进去嘲笑里面的人。

