goch and laugh at those kept inside. People would go and look at the patients as if they were animals in a zoo. But at the Quakeres Island outside Philadelphia, called the Friends' Asylum, Thomas Kirkbride was exposed to something very different, a newer gentler philosophy of how to manage the people in their care. It was called moral treatment. And the starting idea was that the mentally ill person was not responsible for his or her illness. Pioneered by a small handful of the silums in Europe, moral treatment was part of a growing trend among professional physicians who believed that patients didn't have to be confined indefinitely.
人们会去观看病人，就像看动物园里的动物一样。但在费城郊外那家名为“朋友精神病院”的贵格会机构，托马斯·柯克布赖德接触到了非常不同的东西，一种更新、更温和的管理护理对象的方法。这被称为“道德治疗”。其基本理念是，精神病患者不对他或她的疾病负责。道德治疗由欧洲少数几家精神病院首创，是专业医生中日益增长的趋势的一部分，他们认为病人不必被无限期监禁。

